<div class="article-body prose prose-lg dark:prose-invert max-w-none">

  <p>
    The following accounts are illustrative case studies drawn from composite experiences reported by SleepTech users across Japan, Vietnam, and Thailand. Names and identifying details have been changed. They are presented not as individual testimonials but as representative portraits of the emotional and practical realities that families navigating elder care across distance encounter — and of the specific ways in which continuous, non-intrusive monitoring can shift those realities.
  </p>

  <h2>Story One: Keiko and Her Mother in Hokkaido</h2>
  <p>
    Keiko is 47, a product manager at a technology company in Tokyo. Her mother, Fumiko, is 81 and lives alone in the house in Sapporo where Keiko grew up. The distance between them is 831 kilometers by road, or about 90 minutes by plane — a distance that Keiko crosses four or five times a year, on holidays and when her mother's health requires it. In between, there are the phone calls: every evening at 7 PM, almost without exception, for the past eleven years.
  </p>
  <p>
    For Keiko, the phone calls were both connection and burden. "I knew my mother would worry if I missed one, so I never missed one. Even when I was traveling, even when I was exhausted, I called. And in the calls, she always said she was fine. But I started to realize that 'fine' was what she would say regardless of whether she was fine. She was protecting me from worrying." The calls gave Keiko her mother's voice, but not the information behind the voice.
  </p>
  <p>
    Fumiko had fallen once, two years earlier — not seriously, a bruised hip and a badly frightened daughter — but the fall had left Keiko with a specific nighttime anxiety that no amount of reassuring conversation could fully address. "I would wake up at 3 AM sometimes and just lie there thinking: did she get up to use the bathroom? Did she fall? Is she on the floor right now and I won't know until I call at 7?" The scenario she imagined — her mother on the floor, unable to reach the phone, as the hours passed — was not irrational. It had nearly happened once.
  </p>
  <p>
    The YumePad was installed during Keiko's spring visit, with Fumiko's full participation in choosing the settings and the alert thresholds. Fumiko's first response to the setup — "So now you'll know every time I go to the bathroom?" — was met with a careful explanation of exactly what the alerts would and would not show. The conversation shifted something. "My mother said, 'Well, if it means you can sleep, I don't mind.' That sentence has stayed with me. She was already thinking about my sleep, not just her own."
  </p>
  <p>
    For the first two weeks, Keiko found herself checking the app compulsively, every morning before she got up. By the fourth week, she had settled into a pattern: a brief glance at the morning summary (always after her coffee, not before), and the evening call. "The call changed. Before, I was always listening for something wrong in her voice. Now, when she says 'I slept fine,' I actually know what that means. I can see it. It's not a performance for my benefit."
  </p>
  <p>
    Eight months after installation, Fumiko's sleep summary showed a pattern that Keiko had not seen before: two consecutive nights with significantly elevated resting heart rate during sleep and unusually prolonged bed-exit events in the early morning hours. On the third morning, before the 7 PM call, Keiko called her mother at 9 AM. Fumiko had not mentioned it, but she had been experiencing shortness of breath. She saw her doctor that afternoon. The diagnosis was a mild cardiac arrhythmia — manageable with medication, but requiring prompt treatment. "The app didn't diagnose her," Keiko says. "But it gave me a reason to call before she decided not to say anything."
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    <p>"I still call every night. I always will. But now the call is different — it's just love, not reconnaissance."</p>
    <footer>— Keiko, 47, Tokyo (composite case study)</footer>
  </blockquote>

